## HOW A FROZEN TEXT IS READ
Every text stands in a fenced block whose first line is `[[TEXT <id>]]`; the text is every line after the id line up to the closing fence, joined by line feeds, no line feed at its end. Its length and sha256 (UTF-8, unfilled) are in THE FROZEN TABLE; **check every one BEFORE filling; a difference is a STOP.** Fill-ins are `<<NAME>>`, replaced by program only; **no `<<` may remain in anything you write.** `<<WRITE-DATE>>` is `date +%Y-%m-%d` at the write; `<<HHMM>>` the clock of the history write from `date`, once, as `HH:MM`. `<<C-86>>` is P-7's number. `<<NEW-KEY>>` is T-A7's key from your write log (its create or its skip). `<<CMT-57>>` and `<<CMT-1>>` are the comment ids of J-57 and J-1 from your write log. **This kit holds no text alone: a text that fails a check STOPS its step whole** (`STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY <text id> <check>`). Dry-fill once, as the drafter did (THE DRY-RUN FIGURES), and read every filled line whole before Step 4.

